    "exampleHtml": "I <span class=\"vp-verb\">'m</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">working</span> now. · She <span class=\"vp-verb\">is</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">reading</span> a book. · They <span class=\"vp-verb\">aren't</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">playing</span> football.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Use the <strong>present continuous</strong> for something that is happening <em>now</em> or around now: <em>I'm cooking dinner. It's raining. We're learning English this year.</em> Form: <em>am / is / are</em> + verb-<em>ing</em>.",
        "tag": "Now"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Negative</strong>: add <em>not</em>: <em>I'm not working. He isn't working. They aren't working.</em> <strong>Question</strong>: put <em>am / is / are</em> first: <em>Are you working? Is she working? What are you doing?</em> Short answers: <em>Yes, I am. / No, I'm not.</em>",
        "tag": "Negative and question"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Spelling of -ing</strong>: most verbs just add <em>-ing</em> (<em>work → working</em>). Verb ends in <em>-e</em>: drop the e (<em>make → making, write → writing</em>). One short vowel + one consonant: double the consonant (<em>run → running, sit → sitting, stop → stopping</em>). Verb ends in <em>-ie</em>: <em>ie → y</em> (<em>lie → lying</em>).",
        "tag": "Spelling"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Present simple</strong> = routines and facts: <em>I work in a shop. She walks to school every day.</em> <strong>Present continuous</strong> = now, or a short time: <em>I'm working at home today.</em> Time words: <em>always, usually, every day, on Mondays</em> (simple) · <em>now, at the moment, today, this week</em> (continuous).",
        "tag": "Simple or continuous?"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>State verbs</strong> describe what we think, feel or have, not an action. We do not use <em>-ing</em> with them: <em>know, understand, believe, like, love, hate, want, need, prefer</em>. ✓ <em>I know the answer. I want a tea.</em> ✗ <em>I'm knowing. I'm wanting a tea.</em>",
        "tag": "State verbs"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I working now.</em> / <em>I am work now.</em> → ✓ <em>I'm working now.</em> · ✗ <em>I'm wanting a coffee.</em> → ✓ <em>I want a coffee.</em> · ✗ <em>I'm going to school every day.</em> → ✓ <em>I go to school every day.</em> · ✗ <em>She is makeing lunch.</em> → ✓ <em>She is making lunch.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Some verbs can be both: <em>have</em> as a state (own) has no <em>-ing</em> (<em>I have a bike</em>), but <em>have</em> as an action does (<em>I'm having lunch. She's having a shower.</em>). For routines, facts and the full present simple, see <a href=\"/present-simple\">Present simple</a>. The present continuous is also used for plans in the near future (<em>I'm meeting Tom tomorrow</em>), but here we look at the meaning \"now\".",
